Chapter 5B research wave completed without scoring. The attempt directory is read-only, so the cumulative manifest could not be updated on disk; a recovery manifest is included below.

The evidence is mixed. Mexico retained macroeconomic stability and saw improved labor-market poverty indicators in 2022, but inflation, persistent informality, gender gaps, weak countercyclical capacity, and concerns over state favoritism in energy markets materially limit a favorable interpretation. IMF reported broadly neutral fiscal policy, a 3.8% deficit target, untargeted energy subsidies, and policy-rate tightening amid inflation. citeturn22view0 OECD likewise reported fiscal prudence but low tax capacity, weak investment/productivity, and concerns about regulatory stability and competition. citeturn22view1turn22view2 CONEVAL recorded falling labor poverty but noted that it remained above pre-pandemic levels and that gender, rural/urban, and formal/informal gaps persisted. citeturn24view0turn24view3

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22373-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-mexico","claim":"The 2022 recovery was supported by improving labor-market conditions and fiscal policy, while inflation reached 8.7% by August and the central bank raised its policy rate to 9.25%.","locator":"Paragraphs immediately under the Mexico 2022 Article IV summary, lines 247-249.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-01","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22373-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-mexico|lines 247-249|2022 recovery, inflation, and policy-rate response","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral macroeconomic assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF country assessment with period-specific macroeconomic observations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers Mexico’s 2022 economic conditions and policy response.","ruler_attribution":"The fiscal stance and subsidies were government policies under AMLO; monetary-rate decisions were made by the autonomous central bank and should not be attributed personally to the ruler.","contrary_evidence":["The recovery also reflected catch-up momentum and external conditions, so the source does not establish that AMLO’s policies alone caused the improvement."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation—Press Release and Staff Report","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/11/04/mexico-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-525448","claim":"The IMF staff report documented a broadly neutral 2022 fiscal stance, a targeted fiscal-deficit objective of 3.8% of GDP, largely untargeted cost-of-living subsidies, and public-sector debt estimated at about 56% of GDP.","locator":"Report metadata and fiscal-policy findings; report page lines 217-244, with fiscal findings summarized in the associated Article IV materials.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-02","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/11/04/mexico-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-525448|fiscal-policy findings|2022 fiscal stance and subsidies","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"],"source_type":"multilateral staff report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF staff assessment tied to the 2022 Article IV consultation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the target year and evaluates 2022 policy.","ruler_attribution":"Fiscal policy and subsidy design were executive-government choices; the report does not establish AMLO’s personal role in every implementation detail.","contrary_evidence":["The report’s macroeconomic assessment is not a judgment of overall economic competence or distributional fairness."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 1","publisher":"Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-1_62d0ca31-en/full-report/component-36.html","claim":"The OECD projected 1.9% growth and 6.9% inflation for Mexico in 2022, recommended temporary and targeted energy-price support, and emphasized regulatory certainty and protection of existing contracts to support investment.","locator":"Mexico section, lines 3988-3989.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-03","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-1_62d0ca31-en/full-report/component-36.html|lines 3988-3989|growth, inflation, energy support, and regulatory certainty","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.9"],"source_type":"multilateral economic outlook","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country-specific outlook with explicit 2022 projections and policy recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses the 2022 outlook, though some figures are projections rather than final outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"The recommendations concern policies available to the AMLO government; the projection itself is not a ruler-attribution finding.","contrary_evidence":["Projected growth and inflation do not measure implementation quality or household welfare directly."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf","claim":"The OECD found Mexico fiscally prudent but constrained by a very low tax-to-GDP ratio, limited countercyclical capacity, weak investment and productivity, and substantial infrastructure, education, and health needs.","locator":"PDF page 13, lines 595-610 and 662-683; fiscal-policy summary also at PDF pages 68-69, lines 3942-3956.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-04","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf|pp.13,68-69|fiscal capacity, investment, productivity, education, health, and energy infrastructure","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral country survey PDF","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed OECD country survey with explicit policy findings and recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and evaluates structural conditions inherited and present during the AMLO period.","ruler_attribution":"The findings describe the policy environment during AMLO’s administration but distinguish structural constraints from personal conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The report also records fiscal prudence and an improved budgetary process, limiting any one-sided negative interpretation."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Readout of Ambassador Katherine Tai’s Virtual Roundtable on Issues in Energy Sector in Mexico","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-03-25","url":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/march/readout-ambassador-katherine-tais-virtual-roundtable-issues-energy-sector-mexico","claim":"USTR stated that ongoing Mexican government actions were increasing state control and limiting competition in the energy sector, including changes associated with the 2021 electricity law.","locator":"Paragraphs 1-2, lines 72-73.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-05","canonical_fact_key":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/march/readout-ambassador-katherine-tais-virtual-roundtable-issues-energy-sector-mexico|lines 72-73|state control and competition concerns","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.10"],"source_type":"foreign trade-authority statement","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.S. trade authority statement, but it presents an interested party’s interpretation of Mexico’s policy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued during 2022 and concerns policies implemented during the target period.","ruler_attribution":"The statement addresses Mexican government policy under AMLO; personal attribution is indirect because it does not document his individual decision process.","contrary_evidence":["USTR’s institutional interest in protecting U.S. commercial access may influence its framing; this should be read alongside Mexican legal records and independent analysis."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico Overview: Development News, Research, Data","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/mexico/overview","claim":"The World Bank reports that Mexico’s official multidimensional poverty rate fell from 43.2% in 2016 to 36.3% in 2022, lifting 5.4 million people, with labor-market outcomes including minimum-wage increases identified as the main driver.","locator":"Mexico Overview, Results section, lines 23-31.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-06","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/mexico/overview|lines 23-31|2016-2022 multidimensional poverty change","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral country overview","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country-level synthesis based on official poverty measures, but the page is not a contemporaneous 2022 evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Outcome includes 2022 but compares a multi-year period beginning before AMLO’s presidency.","ruler_attribution":"The result is compatible with AMLO-era labor and wage policies but cannot be attributed solely to him because the comparison spans multiple years and many determinants.","contrary_evidence":["The statistic does not establish equal gains across gender, regions, formal/informal workers, or access to services."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pobreza laboral: Cuarto trimestre de 2022","publisher":"Consejo Nacional de Evaluación de la Política de Desarrollo Social (CONEVAL)","publication_date":"2023-02-20","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx","claim":"Between the fourth quarters of 2021 and 2022, national labor poverty fell from 40.3% to 38.5%, real labor income per capita rose 4.3%, and employment increased by 1.7 million, but 2022 labor poverty remained above the pre-pandemic level.","locator":"Results bullets and methodology notes, lines 152-164 and 172-180.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-5B-07","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx|lines 152-180|labor poverty, income, employment, and pre-pandemic comparison","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"national official statistical evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CONEVAL uses INEGI labor-survey data and explains its indicator construction and population-estimate revision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures Mexico in the fourth quarter of 2022 and compares it with 2021 and pre-pandemic conditions.","ruler_attribution":"The outcome occurred under AMLO but is not solely attributable to executive policy; CONEVAL identifies employment and income changes, not a single causal policy.","contrary_evidence":["Labor poverty remained above the first-quarter-2020 level, and inflation raised the food-basket threshold."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

